\title{documenteR: collect, document and export analysis outputs}
\description{
\code{documenteR} turns the scattered \code{ggsave()} / \code{write.csv()} / \code{saveRDS()}
calls of a typical analysis script into a single, documented export step.

The workflow has three stages:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Initialise} a collection with \code{\link[=init_outputs]{init_outputs()}}.
\item \strong{Append} outputs as they are produced with \code{\link[=append_plot]{append_plot()}},
\code{\link[=append_data]{append_data()}}, \code{\link[=append_table]{append_table()}} and \code{\link[=append_stats]{append_stats()}}, documenting
each one inline (title, description, variables, sources, formula).
\item \strong{Export} everything at once with \code{\link[=save_outputs]{save_outputs()}}, which writes a
versioned folder containing the files themselves plus README files, a
machine-readable manifest, a data codebook, a typeset PDF plot
catalogue and a complete run log.
}
}
\section{Design notes}{

An output collection is an \emph{environment} with class
\code{documenteR_outputs}. Environments have reference semantics, so
\verb{append_*()} can add to the collection in place without reassigning it,
which is what makes the "mark things as you go" workflow ergonomic. The
collection still behaves like the nested list it replaces: \code{x$plots},
\code{x$gpars$formats$plots <- ".svg"} and \code{length(x$data)} all work as
expected.

The package deliberately depends only on \code{cli}, \code{rlang} and base R.
Writers for specific file formats (\code{.xlsx}, \code{.parquet}, ...) and
renderers for specific plot systems (\code{ggplot2}, \code{lattice}, ...) are
optional and checked for at the point of use, with an actionable error
if a package is missing.
}

